winemac: the Dock shows a program's largest icon, drawn as a Mac app icon

patches/runtime/0030 done for CrossOver's Mac driver, whose icon code is older and different.

- Only one picture of a program's icon reached the Dock: the list the Windows side hands back is
  on the program's stack, and making the first icon from it reuses that stack, so the entries
  after the first were read as garbage (and their handles leaked). CrossOver hands back the
  256-pixel PNG pictures as pointers into the resource, which survived; the bitmap sizes did not.
  The list is now copied first.
- The program's own icon is read once per process. CrossOver read it again for every new window,
  slowly enough that Helldivers 2 needed a hack (CW Hack #26536), which this replaces.
- A window's icon (WM_SETICON, the class icon) replaces it when it has a larger picture: a whole
  group from whichever module it was loaded from (that module stays loaded, since the PNG
  pictures point into it), or the icon itself if it was made at run time. Windows' stand-ins for
  a window without an icon (IDI_WINLOGO, the Wine logo) and IDI_APPLICATION never do. A larger
  icon arriving after the program is in the Dock is shown at once. CrossOver's own icon for a
  program with none (CrossOver Hack 13440) still applies until one comes.
- The icon is drawn as FineWine draws its programs' (finewine_mac_style_icon, the same code as
  patches/runtime/0030) in place of CrossOver's mask (CW Hack 25964): every size from 16 to 1024
  pixels instead of 512 only, a shape scaled from its visible part rather than an edge heuristic,
  small icons crisp rather than blurred, a square picture filling the rounded square. CrossOver's
  mask is still used if the drawing fails. FINEWINE_MAC_ICONS=0 leaves icons unmasked.
- FINEWINE_APP_ICON_DUMP=<file> writes the Dock icon's largest picture as a PNG when it changes.

Ships as two modules: winemac.so and the Windows half, winemac.drv.

